      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c154f595-399d-597f-805a-21b5e020d40e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Kolozsvár Ghetto, Cluj, Hungary\n- Events: World War I, World War II, Treaty of Trianon, Second Vienna Award\n- Organizations: Allies of World War I\n- States: Romania, Kingdom of Hungary, Austria-Hungary\n\nFacts:\n- In 1920, the Treaty of Trianon formally ended World War I between most of the Allies of World War I and the Kingdom of Hungary, which was a successor state to Austria-Hungary.\n- In 1920, the Treaty of Trianon left Hungary as a landlocked state with an area of 93,073 square kilometers, or 28% of the 325,411 square kilometers of the pre-war Kingdom of Hungary.\n- Between the signing of the Treaty of Trianon in 1920 and the Second Vienna Award in 1940, Cluj was part of Romania.\n- During the World War II era, the Kolozsvár Ghetto was one of the lesser-known Jewish ghettos.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "7f5ccbc9-5b84-567f-bb63-e8415052d88e",
        "text": "Page: Kolozsvár Ghetto. Sentence: The Kolozsvár Ghetto was one of the lesser-known Jewish ghettos of the World War II era.\nPage: Kolozsvár Ghetto. Sentence: Between the signing of the Treaty of Trianon in 1920 and the Second Vienna Award in 1940, Cluj was a part of Romania.\nPage: Treaty of Trianon. Sentence: The Treaty of Trianon was the peace agreement of 1920 to formally end World War I between most of the Allies of World War I and the Kingdom of Hungary, the latter being one of the successor states to Austria-Hungary.\nPage: Treaty of Trianon. Sentence: It left Hungary as a landlocked state covering 93073 sqkm , only 28% of the 325411 sqkm that had constituted the pre-war Kingdom of Hungary (the Hungarian half of the Austro-Hungarian monarchy).",
